  "query_preview": "..\nSatellite has mostly cloudy skies at various heights, dominating\nSoutheast Arizona today. The upper level trough is located over\nthe Four Corners and it will push east to northeast into the \nRockies towards the end of the day. This will bring isolated \nsprinkles to a rain shower across the region, mainly favoring \nthe Sky Islands and the White Mountains. Westerly breezes will \nalso continue with gusts up to 25 to 30 mph. \n\nA minor ridging will build late tonight, bringing a warm and \ndry period for Sunday and Monday. Temperatures will be slightly\nbelow normal tomorrow and near normal on Monday. \n\nSeries of weather systems will continue for the region with\nbreaks between them. Ensemble models show our next weather \nsystem moving in by early Tuesday. It is leaning towards a \ndryer weather system compared to the previous weather systems. \nForecasted rainfall amounts are very little to 0.25\". Plus, \nsouth to southwest breezes can be expected. After, we will\nreturn to drying and slight warming trend. Temperatures will be\nbelow to near normal for majority of the week.\n\nEnsembles models show another system coming through the region\nby the following weekend.",
